<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Little Owl</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<!--
	  Both come from build settings rather than being written here, so that
	  `xcodebuild CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION=...` actually changes the build number.
	  Hard-coded, the TestFlight workflow's override did nothing and every upload
	  claimed to be build 1 - which Apple accepts exactly once and then rejects as a
	  duplicate. MARKETING_VERSION and CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION default to 0.1 and 1
	  in the project, so a local build still has sensible numbers.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>

	<!-- Required for App Store validation when the Info.plist is hand-written. -->
	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
	<string>AppIcon</string>

	<!--
	  No encryption beyond what iOS itself provides. Declared here so TestFlight stops
	  asking the export-compliance question on every single build.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Landscape only, iPad only. -->
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIRequiresFullScreen</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Nothing to read, nothing to dismiss: no status bar, no home indicator. -->
	<key>UIStatusBarHidden</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>

	<!-- Generated launch screen: a plain background, no text a child would have to read. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  Shown on the very first tap of the owl, and never before. Written for the parent
	  standing next to the child, not for the child.
	-->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Little Owl uses the microphone only while your child is talking to the owl, so the owl can repeat what they said in a funny voice. Nothing is recorded, saved or sent anywhere — the sound is kept in memory for a few seconds and then deleted.</string>

	<!--
	  Asked for on the first tap of the lamp, and only once the microphone has already
	  been allowed. Recognition is used to notice that the child spoke, never to grade
	  what they said, and it runs entirely on the device: the request sets
	  requiresOnDeviceRecognition and is never started without it.
	-->
	<key>NSSpeechRecognitionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>When your child says a prayer or rhyme back to the owl, the owl listens just long enough to notice that they spoke, so it can praise them and move on. It never checks whether they said it correctly. Everything happens on this iPad — nothing is recorded, saved or sent anywhere.</string>

	<!--
	  Deliberately absent, and they must stay absent:
	    * NSAppTransportSecurity      - the app makes no network calls of any kind
	    * any third-party SDK keys    - there are no third-party SDKs
	    * NSUserTrackingUsageDescription - nothing is tracked
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
